WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.047
Welcome to part three, the second
unit of exam 1: transmission lines.

2
00:00:06.097 --> 00:00:09.729
Last year, four of the ten
problems came from here:

3
00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:13.402
the input impedance of a
line, a VSWR, a coaxial

4
00:00:13.442 --> 00:00:17.825
cable from a datasheet, and
return loss with cable loss.

5
00:00:17.875 --> 00:00:21.281
We build the theory from the
line model, then solve every

6
00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:24.728
example and problem on the
slides, learn to use the Smith

7
00:00:24.768 --> 00:00:28.685
chart, and finish with S-parameters
and impedance matching.

8
00:00:30.335 --> 00:00:33.852
A transmission line is a physical
medium that carries electrical

9
00:00:33.892 --> 00:00:37.965
energy, in which the voltage and
the current vary along its length.

10
00:00:38.015 --> 00:00:41.930
What matters is the length compared
with the guided wavelength, the

11
00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:46.689
speed of light over f times the square
root of the effective permittivity.

12
00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:50.297
If the line is much shorter than
a wavelength, all points see the

13
00:00:50.337 --> 00:00:53.841
same voltage at the same time and
ordinary circuit theory works;

14
00:00:53.881 --> 00:00:57.494
once it is a sizeable fraction of
a wavelength, as a rule of thumb

15
00:00:57.534 --> 00:01:02.147
more than a tenth, we must treat it as
a line with waves travelling along it.

16
00:01:02.197 --> 00:01:05.674
For example, one metre of
polyethylene coaxial cable is half a

17
00:01:05.714 --> 00:01:10.439
wavelength long at 99 MHz, so it is
a transmission line there, but at

18
00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:15.991
50 kHz it is about a four-thousandth of a
wavelength and behaves as a simple wire.

19
00:01:17.641 --> 00:01:21.016
A short piece of line is
modelled by four parameters per

20
00:01:21.056 --> 00:01:24.613
unit length: a series resistance
R for the conductor losses

21
00:01:24.653 --> 00:01:28.905
and a series inductance L, both
in the direction of the current.

22
00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:31.799
And a shunt conductance
G for the losses in the

23
00:01:31.839 --> 00:01:36.178
dielectric, and a shunt capacitance
C between the two conductors.

24
00:01:36.228 --> 00:01:40.007
Solving the circuit equations
for a chain of these pieces, the

25
00:01:40.047 --> 00:01:44.134
voltage along the line is the sum
of a forward wave, V plus times e

26
00:01:44.174 --> 00:01:48.199
to the minus gamma x, and a reflected
wave, V minus times e to the

27
00:01:48.239 --> 00:01:53.517
plus gamma x; the current has the same
two waves with a minus sign between them.

28
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:57.884
Gamma, the propagation constant,
is the square root of R plus j

29
00:01:57.924 --> 00:02:01.827
omega L times G plus j omega
C, with real part alpha, the

30
00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:06.322
attenuation, and imaginary part
beta, the phase constant; and the

31
00:02:06.362 --> 00:02:10.749
characteristic impedance Z zero,
the ratio of voltage to current

32
00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:16.603
in a single wave, is the square root of
R plus j omega L over G plus j omega C.

33
00:02:18.253 --> 00:02:21.160
In most problems the
line is lossless, R and

34
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.431
G are zero, and everything
becomes simpler.

35
00:02:24.481 --> 00:02:27.179
The characteristic impedance
is the square root

36
00:02:27.219 --> 00:02:31.182
of L over C, a real number
such as 50 or 75 ohms.

37
00:02:31.232 --> 00:02:34.128
The propagation constant
is purely imaginary, j

38
00:02:34.168 --> 00:02:37.376
beta, with beta equal to
omega times the square root

39
00:02:37.416 --> 00:02:41.264
of L C, which is also two pi
over the guided wavelength.

40
00:02:41.314 --> 00:02:44.431
The phase velocity is f
times lambda g, omega

41
00:02:44.471 --> 00:02:48.468
over beta, which is one over
the square root of L C.

42
00:02:48.518 --> 00:02:52.629
The waves travel without
attenuation, only changing phase.

43
00:02:52.679 --> 00:02:56.471
Combining the two formulas gives
a result we will use for coaxial

44
00:02:56.511 --> 00:03:01.458
cables: Z zero is one over the phase
velocity times the capacitance per metre.

45
00:03:03.108 --> 00:03:05.427
When the line ends in a load different

46
00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:08.485
from Z zero, part of
the wave is reflected.

47
00:03:08.535 --> 00:03:12.751
The reflection coefficient is the
ratio of the reflected to the

48
00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:18.139
forward wave, and at the load it is Z
L minus Z zero over Z L plus Z zero.

49
00:03:18.189 --> 00:03:21.066
If the load equals Z
zero, the coefficient is

50
00:03:21.106 --> 00:03:24.762
zero and nothing is reflected:
the load is matched.

51
00:03:24.812 --> 00:03:30.149
Looking into a line of length l terminated
in Z L, the input impedance is Z

52
00:03:30.189 --> 00:03:35.527
zero times Z L plus j Z zero tan beta
l, over Z zero plus j Z L tan beta l;

53
00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:41.439
since the tangent repeats every half
wavelength, so does the input impedance.

54
00:03:41.489 --> 00:03:44.327
Moving back from the load
towards the generator, the

55
00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:47.317
reflection coefficient keeps
its modulus and its phase

56
00:03:47.357 --> 00:03:51.638
decreases by twice beta l, a fact
that is the basis of the Smith chart.

57
00:03:51.688 --> 00:03:55.497
For power, the reflected power
is the modulus of gamma squared

58
00:03:55.537 --> 00:03:59.284
times the incident power, and
the load receives the rest, one

59
00:03:59.324 --> 00:04:03.833
minus gamma squared; maximum power
is delivered when gamma is zero.

60
00:04:05.483 --> 00:04:09.468
The forward and reflected waves
interfere and create a standing-wave

61
00:04:09.508 --> 00:04:14.239
pattern along the line; watch the three
cases, with the load on the right.

62
00:04:14.289 --> 00:04:17.215
With a matched load there
is only the forward wave,

63
00:04:17.255 --> 00:04:20.630
and the envelope of the
voltage, in orange, is flat.

64
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.722
With a partial reflection, here gamma
equal to one half, the envelope

65
00:04:24.762 --> 00:04:29.609
swings between a maximum of one plus
gamma and a minimum of one minus gamma.

66
00:04:29.659 --> 00:04:33.349
The voltage standing-wave
ratio, SWR, is the maximum

67
00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:37.217
over the minimum, one plus
the modulus of gamma over one

68
00:04:37.257 --> 00:04:43.477
minus the modulus of gamma;
here 1.5 over 0.5, which is 3.

69
00:04:43.527 --> 00:04:48.260
Inverting it, the modulus of
gamma is SWR minus one over SWR

70
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:53.200
plus one, the formula you need
in almost every exam question.

71
00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.032
With a short or an open circuit
everything is reflected, the

72
00:04:57.072 --> 00:05:01.110
minima are true zeros and the
SWR is infinite, while a matched

73
00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:06.406
line has an SWR of one; the maxima and
minima repeat every half wavelength.

74
00:05:08.056 --> 00:05:11.007
The slides ask you to discuss
the special cases of the

75
00:05:11.047 --> 00:05:14.443
input impedance formula, and
they are worth memorising.

76
00:05:14.493 --> 00:05:18.312
At low frequency beta l is almost
zero, the tangent vanishes

77
00:05:18.352 --> 00:05:22.819
and the input impedance is just
the load: the line is invisible.

78
00:05:22.869 --> 00:05:26.853
A matched line shows Z zero at
its input whatever its length.

79
00:05:26.903 --> 00:05:30.835
A short-circuited line gives
j Z zero tan beta l, and an

80
00:05:30.875 --> 00:05:34.807
open line minus j Z zero
cotangent beta l: both are pure

81
00:05:34.847 --> 00:05:39.525
reactances, which is how stubs
make inductors and capacitors.

82
00:05:39.575 --> 00:05:43.268
A half-wavelength line
repeats the load exactly.

83
00:05:43.318 --> 00:05:46.697
And a quarter-wavelength line
inverts it, Z zero squared

84
00:05:46.737 --> 00:05:50.116
over Z L, so a short becomes
an open and an open becomes

85
00:05:50.156 --> 00:05:54.048
a short; we will use this in
the quarter-wave transformer.

86
00:05:55.698 --> 00:05:59.623
This question looks hard but is a
one-liner once you see the trick.

87
00:05:59.673 --> 00:06:02.708
Beta l is two pi over
lambda times an eighth of a

88
00:06:02.748 --> 00:06:06.598
wavelength plus n halves, which
is pi over 4 plus n pi; the

89
00:06:06.638 --> 00:06:11.192
n pi does not change the tangent,
so tan beta l is one for every n.

90
00:06:11.242 --> 00:06:17.444
Substituting, the numerator is j Z zero
plus j Z zero, twice j Z zero, and the

91
00:06:17.484 --> 00:06:24.235
denominator is Z zero plus j times j Z
zero, which is Z zero minus Z zero, zero.

92
00:06:24.285 --> 00:06:26.652
So the input impedance is infinite: the

93
00:06:26.692 --> 00:06:29.941
line looks like an open
circuit, whatever n is.

94
00:06:29.991 --> 00:06:34.179
You can confirm it with the reflection
coefficient: for Z L equal to

95
00:06:34.219 --> 00:06:38.407
j Z zero, gamma at the load is j
minus one over j plus one, which is

96
00:06:38.447 --> 00:06:42.200
j, and moving an eighth of a
wavelength towards the generator

97
00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.315
rotates it by 90 degrees clockwise,
to plus one, the open-circuit point.

98
00:06:48.965 --> 00:06:51.614
This is the worked example on the slides.

99
00:06:51.664 --> 00:06:57.885
Z L minus Z zero is minus 35 plus
j 20, and Z L plus Z zero is 115

100
00:06:57.925 --> 00:07:07.002
plus j 20, so the modulus of gamma
is 40.3 over 116.7, which is 0.345.

101
00:07:07.052 --> 00:07:13.448
The SWR is 1.345 over
0.655, 2.06; the slide

102
